**user**:
> I have been collecting PDFs of academic papers and reports for lots of different reasons for many years. Now I have them scattered across many folders. As journals are locking up and institutions are paying for fewer of them, I want to be able to more easily access all the papers I've collected, knowing that I won't always be able to find them online. How can I set up my paper collection so that I can easily grab papers across all those different places?
**assistant**:
> You can certainly organize your collection to streamline access, especially considering the challenges with journal access. Here’s a systematic approach you might find useful:
> 
> 1. **Central Repository**: Consolidate all your PDFs into a single, centralized folder on your computer or cloud storage (like Google Drive, Dropbox, etc.), making them easier to index and search.
> 
> 2. **Use a Reference Manager**: It sounds like Zotero is already a tool you've engaged with before. It's excellent for organizing papers since it can store files and provide a rich set of metadata. Other options include Mendeley and EndNote. These tools allow you to:
> 
>    - **Import PDFs**: Drag and drop your files into the reference manager, which will attempt to automatically retrieve metadata (title, authors, etc.). You can manually edit these entries if any data is missing or incorrect.
>    
>    - **Tag and Group**: Use tags and collections to categorize papers by topic, project, or any other criteria relevant to you, enabling quick retrieval.
> 
> 3. **Structure the File System**: Within your central folder, create sub-folders or use symbolic links for categories or projects, which can reflect the organization in your reference manager. This provides a secondary layer of organization.
> 
> 4. **File Renaming**: Consider using a consistent naming convention for your PDFs, such as `Author_Year_Title.pdf`. Some reference managers can automate this renaming process based on the metadata.
> 
> 5. **Search and Retrieval**: Ensure that your repository is indexed by your operating system’s search tool (like macOS Spotlight or Windows Search), so you can quickly search for keywords within the documents.
> 
> 6. **Backup and Sync**: Regularly back up this centralized repository to avoid data loss. If possible, use a cloud solution with offline sync capability, so you always have access to your papers even without internet access.
> 
> 7. **Cross-Platform Accessibility**: Ensure that you can access your collection on multiple devices by syncing files to your smartphone or tablet, allowing you to consult or read papers on-the-go.
> 
> 8. **Automate Updates**: Set reminders or use automation scripts to periodically add new papers to the reference manager, ensuring that your library remains current.
> 
> By organizing your paper collection in this streamlined way, you'll both safeguard against loss of access and enhance your ability to quickly find and use your collected resources. Would you like help setting up any specific tools or methods from this list?
**user**:
> Let's see if Mendeley can be the answer for me. I have a big collection of papers in Mendeley, but I can't open them with clicking on the links because I've moved the PDFs. Is there an online storage option with Mendeley that lets me keep access to them?
**assistant**:
> Yes, Mendeley offers cloud storage options that can help you maintain access to your papers without worrying about broken links. Here’s how you can set it up:
> 
> ### Mendeley Cloud Storage
> 
> 1. **Mendeley Account**: Ensure you have a Mendeley account. If you don’t, you can sign up for free on their website.
> 
> 2. **Sync Your Library**: 
>    - Once you have your Mendeley desktop application set up, you can sync your library to the cloud. This means that all your papers uploaded to Mendeley will be accessible from any device where you log into your account.
>    - Open Mendeley Desktop and click the **“Sync”** button (usually found in the top left corner).
> 
> 3. **Upload PDFs to Mendeley**:
>    - Even if you have moved the PDFs locally, you can drag and drop the files into Mendeley Desktop. This uploads the papers to Mendeley’s cloud storage, ensuring that the links remain intact.
>    - Mendeley allows you to upload PDFs up to a total of 2 GB for free. If you need more space, you can consider their premium plans, which offer larger storage capacities.
> 
> 4. **Organize Your Library**:
>    - Use folders and tags within Mendeley to keep your papers organized, making them easier to find when you need them.
> 
> 5. **Access from Any Device**:
>    - You can access your papers from the Mendeley web interface or from the mobile app, ensuring that you can always find your research on the go.
> 
> 6. **Backup**: 
>    - Although Mendeley keeps a cloud copy, consider having a local backup of your most important or frequently accessed papers on an external drive or another cloud service.
> 
> ### Alternatives
> If you desire additional options besides Mendeley's storage, you might consider syncing your library with platforms like **OneDrive** or **Google Drive**, which can also serve as a backup solution, but ensure any changes are reflected in Mendeley to avoid link issues.
> 
> If you need assistance with specific features in Mendeley or any other tool options, feel free to ask!
